    rationale: "Design note (kept from the draft): We hold no rule on what k'ọla expands to. The served v4.4 prompt lists it as an elision example, and Ejeba and Wiernik expand it as the linker plus a noun while warning that the k could stand for another word. Some speakers use the same letters for a verb of speaking, others write that verb as one word, and others write the linker plus the noun in full. The answer tells us which expansion speakers give (a verb plus a noun, the linker plus a noun, or one word) and whether they hear the ambiguity the write-up claims. Template B, medium: the write-up's own example of an ambiguous contraction, put to the speakers as a question instead of a rule. In gold the letters k'ọla already carry at least two analyses (speak on ig_bank_gram_020 and ig_bank_lex_027; linker plus noun on ig_bank_gram_029), and v4.4 copied the contracted spelling from one speaker ([king] lẹ k'ọla, àmònẹ lẹ n'eti', ig_bank_gram_020) while three others fused it. v4.4 also scatters k'ọla through template prose ('Ẹnẹ du a k'ọla ñwu', ig_bank_cult_021; 'Egba ki ami ọgijọ a k'ọla, ma a du ítá k'ọla', ig_bank_cult_026). Score: whether the model's expansion names the same words as the speaker's, and whether its sentence uses the same reading. Not a control. Final gate: the drafted note, with its speaker forms, is kept verbatim in bank-final-original-notes.json; it left expectedCulturalContext because the seeder leak-checks that field and factual buckets show it to annotators.",
