    promptId: "ig_v45_expd_005",
    bucket: "orthography",
    text: "Your niece is learning to read Igala from her grandmother's old letters and stops at the written form w'ọla. Spell out for her, in full, the words it is short for, then put those full words into one short Igala sentence of your own. Add one English line: could the w' be short for some other word too?",
    difficultyLevel: "intermediate",
    expectedCulturalContext: "We hold no rule on what w'ọla expands to or where its joint sits: one speaker puts the apostrophe after the w, three write the frame as one word, and three put the apostrophe after its second vowel instead. Because the speaker who writes the apostrophe after the w also puts other subject pronouns before it, the w does not look like the subject pronoun, but we do not know what it is. The answer tells us the parts of the welcome frame and whether its second part is the same word as in k'ọla.",
