    rationale: "Template A, present tense, third person singular, locative. Within the triplet the affirmative line is the control for the negative: it must carry no nasal. Measured failure: v4.4 writes a negative as preverbal á plus a nasal ('Ma á j'ẹñwu ñ', ig_bank_gram_019) where all seven speakers write the bare verb plus one nasal, and it closes a yes-no question with a nasal ('Abọ [house] wẹ ń?', ig_bank_auth_011) where speakers close with a particle ('Abọ akwora [house] wẹ a?' annotator_7, 'ch'ọlafiya ba?' annotator_6, 'Abọ [house] wẹ fá' annotator_8, 'Akwọra [house] wẹ a' annotator_3; annotator_4 edited the retrieval arm's 'Aba abọ [house] wẹ?' to 'Agba abọ [house] wẹ ha?'). annotator_7 also rewrote a nasal-final question as a ba question ('Wẹ gbo ẹnwù kí n ka n?' to 'Wẹ gbo ẹnwù ku ka ba?', ig_v42_gram_028). The model fails measurably if its negative carries á, if its affirmative or question carries a nasal, or if its three lines do not differ where the speaker's do. The scene avoids the father-at-the-farm scenario of ig_v42_gram_037. Routing: annotator_3 and annotator_5 (the two who write corrected forms with reasons) plus annotators 4, 6, 7 and 8, so every speaker's own nasal shape is recorded. Tag: record-ready (the gold is short spoken lines). Final gate: the drafted note, with its speaker forms, is kept verbatim in bank-final-original-notes.json; it left expectedCulturalContext because the seeder leak-checks that field and factual buckets show it to annotators.",
